1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,360
In this tutorial, we'll cover how to resend individual HTTP requests using Burp Repeater,

2
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:19,160
and we'll look at various related workflows and options that you can use to support your testing.

3
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:27,920
In Burp Suite, Repeater is the tool that you can use to resend individual requests,

4
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:32,960
modify them, and issue them over and over.

5
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:39,120
The easiest way to use Burp Repeater is to send requests to it from other tools in Burp.

6
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:46,080
In Burp Suite, you can send requests between Burp tools anywhere that you see them.

7
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:54,080
I'm going to launch Burp's embedded browser and visit a website.

8
00:00:54,080 --> 00:01:01,120
The request is intercepted in Burp proxy, so we can use the context menu and select send to repeater from here.

9
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:06,720
Or if we prefer, we can go to the HTTP history.

10
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:08,960
We click on any requests that we're interested in.

11
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,000
Again, use the context menu, select send to repeater.

12
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:18,880
Or we can go to the target tab and select an item that we're interested in,

13
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:22,640
and do send to repeater from the context menu again.

14
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:26,800
Or from any individual request panel, we can do the same.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,360
So if we send a request to repeater, there it appears.

16
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,560
And on the left hand side, in the request panel, we can see the request and we can also edit it.

17
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:40,640
We can click send to issue the request.

18
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:43,120
And then on the right hand side in the response panel,

19
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:45,760
we can see the response that came back from the server.

20
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:50,640
If we want, we can send the request over and over.

21
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,400
We can edit it and send it again.

22
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:56,640
Change it again.

23
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,000
We can try different things, get different status codes back, different responses.

24
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:07,920
Next to the send and cancel button, we have these back and forward buttons,

25
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:13,280
where we can walk through the history of requests that we've made and see each request and response.

26
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:18,640
And on each button, there's a little drop down where we can jump straight to an item in our history

27
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,200
if we want to send that request again.

28
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:25,200
But repeater can also handle redirects.

29
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:31,680
So if we find a request that caused a redirection response and send that to repeater.

30
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:39,920
Here we can see the server applied with 301 status code, which is a redirection.

31
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,000
And we now have this follow redirection button if we click that.

32
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:49,120
But we'll update the request on the left to be the redirected request.

33
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,240
And it will show the response from that on the right.

34
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:57,280
If we want, we can do this automatically.

35
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:01,600
So if we go to the repeater menu, we have these options for follow redirections.

36
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:09,440
If we turn those on, we hit send this time, but automatically follow the redirect.

37
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,760
And we see the final response on the right.

38
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:17,280
And down at the bottom in the status bar, that tells us that it followed one redirection.

39
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,520
There are some other handy options on the repeater menu.

40
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:27,440
So by default, but repeater will update the content length as you edit requests.

41
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:34,640
If you prefer, you can turn that off by default, but will unpack gzip and deflate content in responses.

42
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:35,920
If you prefer, you can turn that off.

43
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,120
You can tell about whether to process cookies in redirects.

44
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,280
You can change the view of repeater.

45
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:49,760
So you can have top bottom split or other views if you prefer.

46
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,880
You can use hotkeys to send items to burp repeater.

47
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:02,400
So we select an item and type control R or command R on Mac.

48
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:06,080
That request comes into repeater.

49
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,320
We can also use control space or command space to issue the request.

50
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,200
You can configure burp's hotkeys by going to user options,

51
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:19,520
misc, and editing the configured hotkeys here.

52
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,440
If you send multiple requests to repeater,

53
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:32,480
each one will create a different tab in repeater where you can work.

54
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:35,520
So each tab has its own request and response panels.

55
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:36,960
It has its own history.

56
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:39,920
So you can work independently on different requests.

57
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,240
This is really handy in your testing workflow.

58
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:46,080
If you are testing different endpoints of the application, different features,

59
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:50,080
and you want to play with one request for a bit, switch and work on something else,

60
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,480
you can then keep your work and easily switch back.

61
00:04:52,480 --> 00:05:01,360
If you want, you can rename tabs by clicking on the tab header and typing in a different name for the tab.

62
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:06,000
This lets you easily label your tabs so you can come back to them and remember where you left off.

63
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,240
You can reorder tabs by dragging them in the tab header.

64
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:16,160
If you're finished with the tab, you can close it by clicking on the little x.

65
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:24,160
There's one more way to start off with BERT repeater, and that is you can click on these little three dots

66
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:25,840
to create a new tab.

67
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:30,400
And here we're going to say type HTTP because we're working on HTTP requests.

68
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:35,120
If we were working on WebSockets, we would select that option and it would create a different UI

69
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,040
for working on WebSockets messages.

70
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:39,680
We'll cover WebSockets testing in a different tutorial.

71
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:45,920
So if we click on HTTP, we get a new empty tab with no request or response.

72
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:48,160
No target details specified.

73
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:50,320
So we could configure all of these manually.

74
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:52,880
But there's another nice little feature in BERT.

75
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:58,000
If you have a URL that you're interested in, you copy that to the clipboard.

76
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:02,160
You can then select paste URL as request.

77
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:07,680
BERT will then create a request and configure the target details from that URL.

78
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:09,200
And you can then send it.

79
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:15,200
So that's how to resend individual HTTP requests with BERT repeater.

80
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:29,920
And various related workflows and options.

